add('D2.1','Meiotic stage inferred from all four products',
 'A species has 2n = 6. The figure shows chromosome counts in all four products from one meiosis in each of two cells. X produces 4, 4, 2, 2; Y produces 4, 2, 3, 3. In each case one nondisjunction event affects a single chromosome pair or its sister chromatids. Assume all products are recovered and no chromosome is gained or lost by another process.',svg(b,'Chromosome inventories of complete meiotic product sets',h=450),[
 P('Deduce the most consistent stage of nondisjunction in X and Y, explaining the complete pattern rather than a single abnormal product.',A('X is consistent with meiosis I nondisjunction; both homologues enter one first-division cell and neither enters the other.'),R('After the second division all four products are abnormal: two n+1 and two n−1.'),A('Y is consistent with meiosis II nondisjunction in one first-division cell, giving n+1, n−1 and two normal n products.')),
 P('If each product fertilizes a normal gamete, determine the resulting chromosome counts and the fraction of zygotes with the normal count in each case.',A('Normal gametes have 3 chromosomes. X gives zygotes 7, 7, 5, 5; no zygote has 6.'),A('Y gives 7, 5, 6, 6; half have the normal count.')),
 P('Explain why copying DNA before meiosis does not change 2n from 6 to 12.',R('Replication forms sister chromatids joined at each centromere; chromosome number is counted by centromeres rather than DNA strands or chromatids.'))],
 [('normal haploid count',6/2,3),('Y normal fraction',2/4,.5),('X total chromosome count',sum([4,4,2,2]),12)],'SL chromosome tracking and nondisjunction; full recovered-product assumption distinguishes stages. No mitotic-index/cyclin requirement.')

